      <exercise><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a possible graph of a continuous function, <m>f(x)</m>, that satisfies the following properties simultaneously.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> on <m>(-\infty,-8)\cup(3,5)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f'(x) \lt 0</m> on <m>(-8,3)\cup(5,\infty)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f''(x) \lt 0</m> on <m>(-\infty,-3)\cup(1,8)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f''(x) \gt 0</m> on <m>(-3,1)\cup(8,\infty)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(-6)=7</m>, <m>f(1)=0</m>, <m>f(5)=-4</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow -\infty}f(x)=-\infty</m>, <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}f(x)=-9</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            <image source="output/h8fig2" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>A coordinate axis. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                A coordinate axis with <m>x</m>-values ranging from <m>-10</m> to <m>10</m> and <m>y</m>-values ranging from <m>-10</m> to <m>10</m>.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <image source="output/h8fig3" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>A coordinate axis containing a graph of a piecewise function. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                A coordinate axis with <m>x</m>-values ranging from <m>-10</m> to <m>10</m> and <m>y</m>-values ranging from <m>-10</m> to <m>10</m> containing a possible graph of the piecewise function described in the above problem.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            If we are given an <m>x</m>-value <m>x=a</m> and a function <m>f(x)</m> which is differentiable at <m>x=a</m>, the the linearization <m>L(x)</m> represents the linear approximation of <m>f(x)</m> close to <m>x=a</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the linearization of <m>f(x)=\sqrt{x}</m> at <m>a=4</m>. 
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>L(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>L(x)=\dfrac{1}{4}(x-4)+2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Approximate <m>\sqrt{3.95}</m> using linearization.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>\sqrt{3.95} = f(3.95) \approx L(2.1)</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>f(3.95) \approx L(3.95) = 1.9875</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the linearization of <m>g(x)=\dfrac{1}{x}</m> at <m>a=2</m>. 
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>L(x)=g(a)+g'(a)(x-a)</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>L(x)=-\dfrac{1}{4}(x-2)+\dfrac{1}{2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Approximate <m>\dfrac{1}{2.1}</m> using linearization.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>\dfrac{1}{2.1} = f(2.1) \approx L(2.1)</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>g(2.1) \approx L(2.1) = 0.475</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            The radius of a sphere was measured to be <m>5\text{ mm}</m> with a maximum error in measurement of <m>0.06\text{ mm}</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Use differentials to estimate the maximum error in the calculated surface area of the sphere.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>\text{Surface Area} = S = 4\pi r^2</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>\Delta S \approx dS = S'(a) dr</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>dS = 2.4\pi\text{ mm}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              What is the relative error in measuring the surface area of the sphere?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>\text{Relative error} = \dfrac{dS}{S}</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{dS}{S} = 0.024</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              What is the percentage error in measuring the surface area of the sphere?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint> 
            <p>
                <m>\text{Percentage error} = \text{Relative error}\times 100</m>
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\text{Percentage error } = 2.4%</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Find the critical number(s) and relative/local extrema (maxima and minima) of each of the following functions. Be careful to check whether the derivative changes sign by using a sign chart!
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{3}x^3-\dfrac{5}{2}x^2+6x-3</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Critical numbers at <m>x=2,3</m>; Relative minimum at <m>(2,f(2))</m>; Relative maximum at <m>(3,f(3))</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>g(t)=-3t^{\frac{1}{3}}(4-t)^{\frac{2}{3}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Critical numbers at <m>t=0,\dfrac{4}{3},4</m>; Relative minimum at <m>\left(\dfrac{4}{3},g\left(\dfrac{4}{3}\right)\right)</m>; Relative maximum at <m>(4,g(4))</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h(\theta)=\theta e^{-\sqrt[3]{\theta}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Critical numbers at <m>\theta=0,27</m>; No relative minimum; Relative maximum at <m>(27,h(27))</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
